From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 03:57:03 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 34/42] hw/usb/hcd-imx21: Disable an ETD's DMA when its channel
 is cleared

Writing an ETD's bit to ETDDMACHANLCLR now also clears its ETDDMAEN bit,
as well as rewinding the DMA buffer pointer.

Linux's imx21-hcd clears the channel when it retires an ETD and enables
DMA for the ETD's next use with a read-modify-write of ETDDMAEN. With the
stale enable still set, that write toggled the ETD's DMA off, so the data
stage of a control IN went to DMEM instead of memory and enumeration
failed with a garbage device descriptor. The stock 2.6.16 driver clears
the channel just before it enables DMA, so it is unaffected.

Signed-off-by: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
---
 hw/usb/hcd-imx21.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/usb/hcd-imx21.c b/hw/usb/hcd-imx21.c
index 64c260162..f05e7e1d2 100644
--- a/hw/usb/hcd-imx21.c
+++ b/hw/usb/hcd-imx21.c
@@ -770,6 +770,13 @@ static void imx21_usbotg_write(void *opaque, hwaddr offset, uint64_t val,
         R(ETDENSET) &= ~val;
         break;
     case ETDDMACHANLCLR:
+        /*
+         * Clearing an ETD's DMA channel also disables its DMA. Linux's
+         * imx21-hcd relies on that: it clears the channel when it retires
+         * an ETD and later enables DMA with a read-modify-write of
+         * ETDDMAEN, which would otherwise toggle a stale enable off.
+         */
+        R(ETDDMAEN) &= ~val;
         for (i = 0; i < NUM_ETD; i++) {
             if (val & (1u << i)) {
                 s->regs[(ETDDMABUFPTR0 / 4) + i] =
